- Decide the structure of the json files.
    Chosen Idea: {
        Questions = {
            Contextual : [
                { Stuff about the question }
            ],
            NonContextual : [
                { Stuff about the question }
            ]
        },
        Content : [
            { Units? -> Topics? -> Concepts etc? }
        ]
    }
    o Stuff about the question could include:
        0 Question -> what is being asked to do
        0 Model Answer -> suggested answer that would get full marks
        0 Key Words -> what the answer requires to get full marks
        0 Type? -> Could describe in what way the question is meant to be answered (e.g. diagram, definition, evaluation, practical etc.)
        0 Context? -> scenario details for a context question

    o Content will likely entail:
        0 Units -> Names of the key areas of the subjects (e.g. Cybersecurity, Programming, Data Management etc)
        0 Topics -> Things explored in that unit (e.g. Network security, OOP, Databases etc)
        0 Concepts etc -> Base level content that needs to be known and understood (e.g. TCP/IP model, classes, SQL querying)

    o Tags might also be very important so that a user can select questions that belong to a specific tag group:
        0 #Programming or #AustralianPrivacyPrinciples for example,
        0 and they only get questions that either associate with one or more of the tags.
        0 Questions have tags so that it is clear what content is assessed, and content groups have tags so that content can be found.

